  "summary": "I build low-latency distributed systems that evaluate, rank, and execute under strict time constraints.\n\nAt Uber, I work on the end-to-end real-time ad serving pipeline—orchestrating candidate retrieval, ML scoring passes, auction execution, and budget pacing inside a sub-50ms SLA. The engineering profile mirrors high-frequency execution: high-throughput fan-out, strict tail-latency budgets, and high-concurrency state management under hard operational deadlines.\n\nPreviously, I worked on processing order data streams at DoorDash, computing rolling volatility, volume, and latency metrics across sliding windows. At AWS, I worked on the serverless storage layer for Keyspaces, scaling to serve 5M+ requests per second in production.\n\nOn my own time I built a cross-sectional statistical arbitrage engine on high-cap crypto — an Elastic Net alpha model over microstructure features, OLS orthogonalization to strip out market beta, and a regime-switching overlay that held realized beta to 0.18. Out-of-sample Sharpe of 1.30 net of transaction costs.\n\nUC Berkeley, Mathematics and Computer Science. USAMO qualifier. First place, Berkeley Data Fest (sponsored by Google).",
